From ed61877b285cc3f1fff65e6e04c101cbe5150a2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 07:54:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] skyforge(mgmt-runtime): materialize pod IP into
 Management0 startup-config (config-as-truth)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When the CR maps eth0 to an EOS management interface (netlab-authored intent,
faithfully translated into EosIntfMapping.json since the mgmt-faithful series),
cEOS claims eth0 and ENFORCES its config at apply time: a startup-config with no
Management0 stanza reads as "no ip address", so EOS strips the CNI-assigned pod
IP and every kernel route (live evidence 2026-07-18, cEOS 4.34.2f) — all mgmt
(SSH/eAPI/gNMI) goes externally dark. Upstream never hits this because upstream
never claims eth0 at all: KNE's own CLI path is `kubectl exec ... Cli`, its
example configs carry no Management0 stanza, and pristine-operator pods leave
eth0 to the CNI. The moment a Management mapping exists, the config must carry
the runtime identity — exactly how containerlab solves the same problem by
templating the mgmt IP + route into ceos.cfg.

The pod IP is assigned at sandbox creation, AFTER the operator renders the pod
and configmaps, so the only honest place to bind it is inside the pod at start:
the startup-config-seed init container now (when the CR maps eth0->ManagementN)
reads status.podIP via the downward API and refreshes a marker-delimited block
appended to the persisted startup-config:

    ! SKYFORGE-RUNTIME-MGMT BEGIN ...
    interface Management0
       mtu <observed /sys/class/net/eth0/mtu, clamped 68..9214>
       ip address <podIP>/<derived prefix>     e.g. 198.18.0.132/15
    ip route 0.0.0.0/0 Management0
    ip route <cidr> Management0                per SKYFORGE_MGMT_RETURN_CIDRS,
                                               minus the connected one
    ! SKYFORGE-RUNTIME-MGMT END

EOS's enforcement then INSTALLS the address/routes instead of stripping them
(config-as-truth; enforcement is one-shot at claim/config-apply, verified live:
a manually restored addr + `default dev eth0 scope link` route sticks and makes
SSH/eAPI reachable).

Mask decision — live-validated on canary br1 (cEOS 4.34.2f, 2026-07-18): EOS
REJECTS "ip address <ip>/32" with "% Prefix length must be less than 32" (a
rejected line would abort config replay), /31 carries point-to-point pair
semantics, so the prefix is DERIVED at pod start: the prefix length of the
first return CIDR that contains the pod IP (198.18.0.132 in 198.18.0.0/15 ->
/15; pure busybox-sh containment math, (ip XOR base) >> (32-plen)), bounded
/16 fallback when nothing matches — never /32. Canary confirms the full chain:
/15 address accepted, interface-form statics accepted and rendered to kernel
exactly as designed ("default dev eth0 proto gated scope link src <ip>",
"100.64.0.0/10 dev eth0 proto gated"), and SSH/eAPI OPEN through the KNE
Service under the EOS-authored state. The containing CIDR arrives as a
proto-kernel CONNECTED route once the address is up, so it gets no static twin
(avoids static-vs-connected ambiguity); the default route and all other return
CIDRs stay interface-form statics. Interface-form routes are valid EOS grammar
(destinations treated as directly connected, resolved per-destination by ARP —
which the CNI answers) and deliberately embed NO gateway: after `write memory`
absorbs the block into the body, stale route lines are IP-free (config
set-semantics dedupe) and a later `ip address` replaces an earlier one on
replay, so a rescheduled pod with a new IP still converges. Strip-then-append
on every start keeps exactly one block. Return-CIDR routes pin mgmt client
ranges to the management interface so fabric routing learned later (BGP in the
default VRF) cannot hijack replies.

The postStart hook drops its gateway-discovery kernel-route half — it could
never survive the claim (post-strip there is no gateway left to discover), and
the return path is EOS-owned now. The iptables ingress permits stay: EOS offers
no config surface for its control-plane firewall here, and the permit loop must
keep re-asserting while EOS (re)programs its chains during boot.

No CRD schema change (api/ stays pristine); no netlab change (it keeps authoring
intfmapping only). Per-device kill switch: spec.envvars
SKYFORGE_MGMT_RUNTIME_CONFIG=off. Downward-API env lives ONLY on the seed init
container: the reconciler's env drift check compares the ceos container's
literal env values, and a valueFrom var there would read back empty and force a
delete/recreate loop. The seed/active file paths are env-overridable solely so
the unit tests execute the script for real (the curated seed-container env never
sets them in a pod).

