 # cEOS enforces an EOS control-plane firewall: the iptables INPUT policy is DROP
 # and the EOS_INPUT chain starts empty, so every inbound management packet from
 # the Kubernetes pod / Service network (where the analysis system collector pod and the
-# per-device Services it dials both live) is dropped before EOS processes it. The
-# return routes above only correct the reply *path*; they do not lift this
-# ingress filter (a route to the source CIDR does not create a permit). Without
-# an explicit permit the collector's SSH/gNMI SYNs — and even ICMP echo replies —
-# are silently dropped, which is the cEOS-on-Talos "collector cannot reach the
-# device" regression. Permit each management source CIDR explicitly. The
-# delete-then-insert keeps exactly one ACCEPT per CIDR across the retry loop,
-# ordered ahead of the default DROP.
+# per-device Services it dials both live) is dropped before EOS processes it.
+# Without an explicit permit the collector's SSH/gNMI SYNs — and even ICMP echo
